From c92e00131ff08245cae258a22b7b1ae42b302a8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:36:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] feat(deploy): send resource events to a webhook on
 Kubernetes

The control plane can now POST resource events to a webhook, but the
Kubernetes manifests had no way to set the FORGEMESH_EVENT_WEBHOOK_*
variables, and the default policies only let the control plane reach
the cluster DNS, so every delivery would time out until the events
went dead.

event-webhook/patch.yaml sets the URL, the timeout and the token. The
token comes from the forgemesh-event-webhook Secret through an
optional reference, so the patch also works without a token and the
value never appears in the Deployment. event-webhook/network-policy.yaml
adds the egress rule for the receiver. A NetworkPolicy cannot match
host names, so the file carries one rule for an in-cluster receiver
(namespace, pod labels and container port) and one for an external
receiver (an address block), both with placeholder values, and the
README explains which one to keep, how to go through an egress proxy
when the receiver's addresses are not stable, and how to redrive dead
events with kubectl exec.

Validated on kind with the patch (only the URL edited) and a token
Secret created from a file that ends with a newline: every delivery
timed out until the egress policy was applied unmodified for a
receiver behind a Service on port 80 with target port 8080. The
retries then arrived with the bearer token, later events arrived on
the first attempt, and the redrive command ran through kubectl exec.
